  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nI will implement a novel heuristic approach based on probability density approximation and stochastic projection. The core idea is to model the step function as a probability density f(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted version. The rationale is that:\n- Probability density functions are well-established in optimization literature\n- This approach can more naturally handle the integral constraint than the previous grid-based methods\n- The density function formulation allows for continuous optimization\n- The method leverages the structure of the overlap integral to guide the search\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is the high dimensionality and computational expense of the coordinate descent approach, handled by the probability density approximation.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables: \n- Probability density f(x): [0,2] \u2192 \u211d\u207a\n- Target integral constraint: \u222bf(x)dx = 1 (equivalent to \u2211f_i * dx = 1)\n\nObjective:\n- Minimize max\u2096 (1/(2k)) \u222b\u222b f(x)f(y)1_{|x-y|\u2264k} dx dy dy (discretized)\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 f_i \u2264 C_i for all i\n- \u2211f_i = 2 (resulting from \u222bf(x)dx=1 and dx=2/n)\n\nRepresentation:\n- f_i = probability masses at each grid point\n- C_i = large positive constant (like 1000) ensuring pseudo-positivity\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialization\n- Set C = 1000 (upper bound) and n_plank = 1000 (grid size for extrapolation)\n- Initialize f_i = C/n for all i\n- Compute baseline C5 value\n\nb. Density Modification\nFor each iteration:\n1. Compute statistical moments: mean, variance, skewness\n2. Identify k_shift from worst shift (highest overlap)\n3. Calculate adaptive learning rate \u03c3 based on current deviation\n4. Update distribution:\n   f_i \u2190 f_i * exp(-\u03c3 * (distance to k_shift)^2)\n5. Renormalize to satisfy \u2211f_i=2\n6. Clip values to [0, C]\n\nc. Thresholding\nAfter each adaptation:\n1. Identify sparse elements (very small f_i)\n2. Replace with uniform distribution over sparse regions\n\nd. Validation\n- Ensure sum(f_i) \u2248 2 with tolerance 1e-6\n- Maintain 0 \u2264 f_i \u2264 C\n\ne. Optimization Loop\n- Set T_sweeps=5000 (max iterations)\n- Set time limit for each phase proportional to n_plank\n- Use adaptive \u03c3 based on distance from mean\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\na. Initial configuration:\n   - C = 1000\n   - n_plank = 1000\n   - T_sweeps = 5000\n   - Initial adaptation strength \u03c3_init=0.1\n\nb. Parameter settings:\n   - \u03c3_adaptive = 1.0 + 0.5*sqrt(|mean_deviation|) \n   - Sparsity threshold: eps=1e-4\n   - Initialization iterations: 100\n\nc. Critical decisions:\n   - Scale adaptation by gradient information only for large overlaps\n   - Use alternating adaptation in k-space and x-space\n   - For repeated patterns, introduce mode-seeking parameters\n\n5. Computation Budget and Robustness\nEstimate time distribution:\n- Initialization: O(n_plank)\n- Main loop: O(T_sweeps * n_plank)\n- Adaptive phase: O(n_plank)\n- Validation: O(n_plank)\n\nFeasible schedule:\n1. Initialization phase: 5% budget\n2. Main optimization: 85% budget\n3. Adaptive refinement: 10% budget\n\nTermination conditions:\n- Exceeded iterations scan_threshold=30\n- Relative improvement below 1e-5\n- Spurious mode detection\n\nFallback solution:\n1. If divergence, reinitialize f_i with multiplicative noise\n2. If non-progression, reduce \u03c3 by factor of 0.2\n3. If stuck, perform grid interpolation from coarser grid\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\na. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 \u2264 f_i \u2264 C for all i\n   - sum(f_i) \u2248 2 (absolute error <1e-4 * C)\n   - C5 evaluation uses same discretization as previous\n\nb. Evaluation:\n   - C5 = dx * max\u2096 integral(f(x)(1-h(x+k)))\n   - Use project_feasible to ensure form constraints\n\nc. Fall-back properties:\n   - Guaranteed integral \u2248 n/2\n   - Values confined to [0,1] after normalization\n   - Smooth adaptation path from initialization\n\nImplementation note: The density representation naturally handles the integral constraint by the adjustment factor C. The clip-normalize-projection workflow ensures pseudocode validity in all transformations.",
